#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055614} :activity/id #uuid "c3665ee3-8127-5934-8cf7-8043de2f83a9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049705 :topic/id #uuid "0ce09933-6f9c-542f-8c51-4a31064b55d0" :topic/math-academy-id 131} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055977 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we want to find the probability that two or more events co-occur (occur together). Events like this, involving a combination of two or more events, are called **compound events**.\n\nFor instance, the $100$ members of a volleyball club were asked about their preferences between Chinese food and Italian pizza. The results of the survey are represented in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Chinese food | Italian pizza | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Male | $20$ | $35$ | $55$ |\n| Female | $20$ | $25$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $40$ | $60$ | $100$ |\n\nSuppose that we want to find the probability that a randomly selected club member is a male that prefers pizza. \n\nFirst, let $A$ be the event that a randomly selected member is **\"Male\"** and let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected member prefers **\"Italian pizza\"**. \n\nThe probability that $A$ *and* $B$ co-occur, denoted by $P(A\\cap B)$ and called the **intersection**, is found by identifying the intersection of these events in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the column **\"Italian pizza\"** and the row **\"Male\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Chinese food | Italian pizza | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Male | $20$ | $\\color{blue}35$ | $55$ |\n| Female | $20$ | $25$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $40$ | $60$ | $100$ |\n\nDividing this number by the total number of data points in the table $(100),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{35}{100} \\\\  & =0.35.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the experimental probability that a randomly selected member prefers Italian pizza and is male." :tutorial/id #uuid "5feb6681-bfdb-5bfb-9230-a4f34bbd80ad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3364 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e7ae61c2-30a3-54cf-88c5-c1bbc9d77e99" :step/math-academy-id 18718 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055616}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054661 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054551 :question/id #uuid "671da5f4-e539-588e-9bec-81c71e016d2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5104"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac12981c-ec1d-5f2d-8699-a03bd7d85e14" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559833}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Probability of the Union of Events"} :step/id #uuid "d567d447-5c82-574d-9c7c-79e70871c3e5" :step/math-academy-id 18719 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055617}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054662 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054552 :question/id #uuid "ec0a90ec-7cd7-5ab5-abb2-9e33cbd4b711" :question/math-academy-id "e-5105"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46546a09-5d48-57d9-8166-5f9d8949c066" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048905}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559875}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Probability of the Intersection of Events"} :step/id #uuid "52d226ea-739d-53c5-8fc5-a4d45df4a932" :step/math-academy-id 18720 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055663}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055617 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054663 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054553 :question/id #uuid "f09f68d2-f44f-5328-963b-41a9884a944a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5106"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6956b58-5581-5221-8d4f-88ebde382f0c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559867}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating Using Probabilities"} :step/id #uuid "c37ad3ca-2c39-5b1c-849a-72d3c40e8f36" :step/math-academy-id 18721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055663 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055990 :tutorial/content "Let's return to our first example, where the Volleyball club members were asked about whether they preferred Italian pizza or Chinese food. The data is shown below.\n\n|  | Chinese food | Italian pizza | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Male | $20$ | $35$ | $55$ |\n| Female | $20$ | $25$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $40$ | $60$ | $100$ |\n\nSuppose we want to find the probability that a randomly selected club member either prefers Chinese food *or* is female (or both).\n\nLet $A$ be the event that a randomly selected member prefers **\"Chinese food\"** and let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected member is **\"Female\"**.\n\nThe probability that $A$ *or* $B$ (or both) occurs, denoted by $P(A\\cup B)$ and called the **union**, is found by counting the number of data points in which $A$ or $B$ occurred and dividing by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers that are in *either* the column **\"Chinese food\"** *or* the row **\"Female\"** (or both) are highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Chinese food | Italian pizza | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Male | $\\color{blue}20$ | $35$ | $55$ |\n| Female | $\\color{blue}20$ | $\\color{blue}25$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $40$ | $60$ | $100$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(100),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =\\frac{20+20+25}{100} \\\\  & =\\frac{65}{100} \\\\  & =0.65.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the experimental probability that a randomly selected member prefers Chinese food or is female." :tutorial/id #uuid "098069a2-2cbc-5491-b9de-52d1e84a32c8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4631 :tutorial/title "The Union of Two Events"} :step/id #uuid "cd4e2ae0-2688-5445-855e-0aa2d0de7087" :step/math-academy-id 28504 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055615}}] :activity/title "Compound Events in Probability From Experimental Data" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054881}]}
